ga4c(){return"On the Beat or Off?"},
gary(){return"Straight rhythm, or syncopated?"},
gaxd(){return"Is this rhythm on the beat, or syncopated?"},
gaxe(){return"On the beat"},
gaxf(){return"Syncopated"},
ga4l(){return"Even or Triplet?"},
garH(){return"Is the beat split in two, or in three?"},
gbom(){return"How is the beat split?"},
gbol(){return"Even (2)"},
gbon(){return"Triplet (3)"},
ga3H(){return"Which Ornament?"},
gara(){return"Read the trill, mordent or turn"},
gbb2(){return"Which ornament is on the note?"},
gbb3(){return"Trill"},
gbb1(){return"Mordent"},
gbb4(){return"Turn"},
gbf1(){return"On the beat, or off?"},
gbf0(){return"Usually the notes land right ON the beats \u2014 count 1-2-3-4 and each note lands on a number. Steady and square."},
gbf_(){return'Syncopation pushes notes OFF the beat, onto the "and" in between. The accent lands where your ear didnt expect it \u2014 thats the kick you feel in pop and jazz.'},
gbfn(){return"Two or three in a beat"},
gbfl(){return'Normally a beat splits into TWO even halves: "1-and". Two eighth notes.'},
gbfm(){return'A triplet squeezes THREE equal notes into that same beat: "trip-o-let". It gets a little 3 above it.'},
gbei(){return"Decorating a note"},
gbej(){return"Ornaments are little signs that dress up a note. A trill (tr) shakes quickly between the note and the one just above it."},
gbek(){return"A turn (a sideways S) curls AROUND the note: the note above, the note, the note below, then back. A mordent is just one quick flick up and back."},
ga30(){return"Label the Form"},
gaqz(){return"Hear the sections; name the shape (ABA\u2026)"},
gb0i(){return"What is the form? (same colour = same tune)"},
gb0h(){return"Listen"},
gbdm(){return"The shape of a piece"},
gbdl(){return"Music is built from sections. Here is a little tune \u2014 call it section A. Whenever it comes back, it is A again."},
gbdk(){return"A different tune is a new letter \u2014 section B. Tune, different tune, then the first tune again makes the form A-B-A. Lots of songs are shaped this way!"},
ga3s(){return"Which Mode?"},
gar0(){return"Major, minor or Dorian?"},
gb8T(){return"Listen! Which mode is it?"},
ga00(){return"Major"},
ga01(){return"Minor"},
gb8S(){return"Dorian"},
gbee(){return"Three colours of scale"},
gbec(){return"A major scale sounds bright and happy. Listen to it climb."},
gbed(){return"A minor scale sounds darker \u2014 its 3rd, 6th and 7th steps sit a little lower."},
gbeb(){return"Dorian is like minor, but its 6th step is raised \u2014 so it sounds minor with a brighter twist. That one note is the whole secret!"},
gamS(){return"Textbook"},
gbkr(){return"Read"},
gbko(){return"Work through music from the very start. Each topic has a short lesson (see it, hear it) and games to practise it."},
